+## 2. Datagram transport, crypto and roaming
+
+### 2.1 Wire format [src, verified byte by byte in the transport note]
+
+```
+UDP payload = 8B nonce (bit63 = direction, low 63 bits = seq)
+            + AES-128-OCB( 2B timestamp | 2B timestamp_reply | fragment ) + 16B tag
+fragment    = 8B instruction id | 2B (final<<15 | frag_num) | slice of zlib(protobuf Instruction)
+Instruction = { protocol_version, old_num, new_num, ack_num, throwaway_num, diff, chaff(0–16B) }
+```
+
+Overhead is `Connection::ADDED_BYTES = 12` (8 nonce + 4 timestamps), plus `Session::ADDED_BYTES = 16` (tag), plus `frag_header_len = 10`.
+
+### 2.2 Crypto [src crypto.cc, ocb_openssl.cc]
+
+- **Cipher.** AES-128-OCB with a 12-byte nonce (4 zero bytes followed by a big-endian u64) and a 16-byte tag, with no associated data. The default backend is OpenSSL EVP. Nettle, CommonCrypto and a bundled `ocb_internal.cc` are alternatives.
+- **One key, two directions.** Both directions share the key. The direction bit keeps the two nonce spaces disjoint. The receiver also `dos_assert`s the expected direction, which stops a packet from being reflected back to its sender.
+- **Sequence numbers.** `seq` comes from the process-wide counter `Crypto::unique()`, which is fatal on wrap. The session also dies after 2^47 blocks, from the OCB bound halved because the key is shared.
+- **Failure handling.** A failed tag throws a non-fatal `CryptoException`, so garbage is dropped silently. Packets shorter than 24 bytes are rejected.
+- **Key hygiene.** Core dumps are disabled so the key never reaches disk.
+- **Chaff.** 0–16 random bytes per Instruction (`CHAFF_MAX`) blur length side channels.
+- **Compression.** The whole Instruction is zlib-compressed.
+
+### 2.3 Replay protection through idempotency [paper §2.2, src]
+
+There is no replay window. Every datagram means "diff from numbered state A to numbered state B", so replaying one is harmless [paper: "does not need to maintain a replay cache"].
+
+- **Datagram layer.** Packets with `seq < expected_receiver_seq` are still passed to the transport. They are **not** used for RTT samples or for retargeting the peer address. The source flags this as "security-sensitive".
+- **Transport layer.** An Instruction is dropped if its `new_num` is already held, or if its `old_num` base state is missing ("part of how we enforce idempotency").
+
+### 2.4 Fragmentation and MTU [src network.h/.cc, transportfragment.cc]
+
+**Resolved contradiction.** The SSP note listed `DEFAULT_IPV4_MTU = DEFAULT_IPV6_MTU = 1280` and left open how this is applied at runtime. The transport note said 1280 − 28. Re-checking `Connection::set_MTU()` settles it:
+- IPv4: `MTU = DEFAULT_IPV4_MTU - IPV4_HEADER_LEN`, which is **1252** (the transport note gives IPV4_HEADER_LEN as 28, i.e. IP plus UDP).
+- IPv6: `MTU = DEFAULT_IPV6_MTU - IPV6_HEADER_LEN`, which the transport note gives as **1216**.
+- On `sendto` failing with `EMSGSIZE`: MTU falls back to `DEFAULT_SEND_MTU = 500` ("payload MTU of last resort"). Confirmed at network.cc:384.
+- Per-fragment payload on IPv4 is therefore 1252 − 12 − 16 − 10 = **1214 B**.
+
+Other MTU behaviour:
+- **PMTUD is disabled** (`IP_PMTUDISC_DONT`): DF is cleared, and the comment says "fragmentation is not dangerous, just inefficient". RFC 4821 PLPMTUD is noted as future work.
+- **Unverified:** behaviour on macOS/BSD, where `IP_MTU_DISCOVER` does not exist.
+- `RECEIVE_MTU = 2048`. A truncated datagram throws.
+
+Fragment rules:
+- A 15-bit fragment number caps one Instruction at 32767 fragments (`fatal_assert`).
+- `FragmentAssembly` holds **one in-progress instruction id**. A fragment with a newer id discards the partial assembly.
+- The id changes only when the header fields, chaff or MTU change. Pure retransmits of the same (old, new) pair therefore reuse the fragments and can complete an earlier partial assembly.
+
+### 2.5 Roaming and port hopping [src recv_one, Connection::send/hop_port; paper; doc]
+
+**Server-side roaming.**
+- Any authentic packet with a new highest `seq` retargets `remote_addr` to that packet's source address:port. The source comments "only client can roam".
+- The client does not need to know that its public IP changed.
+- An off-path attacker cannot forge the tag. An on-path attacker replaying a captured packet from a new address fails because its seq is not new.
+
+**Server binding.** The server binds exactly one socket and never hops (`assert(fd_list.size() == 1)`).
+
+**Client port hop.**
+- Trigger: `now − last_port_choice > 10 s` and `now − last_roundtrip_success > 10 s` (`PORT_HOP_INTERVAL = 10000`). The client then opens a fresh socket, which gives it a new ephemeral port and a new NAT mapping.
+- Up to `MAX_PORTS_OPEN = 10` sockets are kept and all of them are polled.
+- Old sockets are dropped once the new one has been current for `MAX_OLD_SOCKET_AGE = 60 s`.
+- "Round-trip success" is fed in from the transport layer (`get_sent_state_acked_timestamp`).
+
+**Heartbeat.** An empty ack every 3 s (`ACK_INTERVAL`). It keeps NAT bindings alive and lets the server learn a roamed address.
+
+**Server detach.** After `SERVER_ASSOCIATION_TIMEOUT = 40 s` of silence the server sets `has_remote_addr = false` and sleeps until a packet arrives.
+
+### 2.6 Ports, NAT and firewalls
+
+- **Resolved discrepancy on the port range.** Source and FAQ say **60001–60999**, with a linear scan for the first free port. The man page and site usage text say 60000–61000. The source is authoritative. The difference does not matter for the usual `60000:61000` firewall rule.
+- **Options.** `-p PORT[:PORT2]` sets the port or range, and `-p 0` lets the OS choose. `-i IP` / `--bind-server` controls the bind address.
+- **Reachability.** The server must accept inbound UDP. The client can sit behind NAT. There is no TCP fallback, relay, hole punching or ProxyJump (#13 open since 2012; #970 open) [issue].
+
