+## 3. SSP state synchronization and frame pacing
+
+### 3.1 Sender state [src transportsender{.h,-impl.h}]
+
+- **`sent_states`** is a list of {timestamp, num, state}.
+  - The front is the **acked** receiver state.
+  - The back is the **last-sent** state.
+  - The iterator `assumed_receiver_state` points somewhere in between.
+- **Optimistic diff base.** `update_assumed_receiver_state()` walks forward from the front. It accepts each state sent less than `timeout() + ACK_DELAY` ago as "probably received". Diffs are computed **from the assumed state**, so the sender can pipeline frames without waiting a full RTT. When the assumption expires, the base falls back to the acked state, and the next diff repairs any loss.
+- **Prospective resend optimization.** The sender also computes the diff from the acked front. It sends that diff instead when it is no larger, or when it is under 1000 B and at most 100 B larger. The packet then doubles as a free retransmit.
+- **Bounds.**
+  - `rationalize_states()` trims acked keystroke prefixes. It is a no-op for the screen.
+  - `sent_states` is capped at 32. Overflow erases the state 16th from the end, keeping the acked front and the recent tail.
+- **Numbering.**
+  - A retransmit of an unchanged state reuses `back().num` and only refreshes the timestamp. A new state gets `back().num + 1`.
+  - **Empty acks also mint a new number.**
+  - Shutdown sends `new_num = UINT64_MAX` up to 16 times, giving up after 10 s.
+
+### 3.2 Receiver logic [src networktransport-impl.h Transport::recv]
+
+1. Reassemble the fragments and check the protocol version.
+2. Process `ack_num`. Acks ride on every packet in both directions.
+3. Feed round-trip success to the datagram layer, which uses it for port hopping.
+4. If `new_num` is already held, drop the packet. This makes replays no-ops.
+5. If the `old_num` base is not held, drop the packet.
+6. `process_throwaway_until(throwaway_num)`: the sender promises never to diff from older states again, so they are discarded.
+7. Flow limit: with more than 1024 states held, refuse new ones for a 15 s quench period. The source's reasoning: "we don't want to ACK a state and then discard it later".
+8. Apply the diff to a copy of the base and insert the result in sorted position. **Only a state appended at the tail advances `ack_num`.** An out-of-order state is stored but not acked as newest.
+9. The application consumes `back().diff_from(last_receiver_state)`. The server writes the resulting `UserStream` to the PTY, collapsing consecutive resizes into one.
+
+Consequences:
+- **No head-of-line blocking.** Any packet whose base is present can be applied immediately.
+- **Loss heals itself.** It is not repaired by byte-exact retransmission.
+
+### 3.3 Frame skipping [src mosh-server.cc serve(), stmclient.cc; paper]
+
+- **Server.** It reads the PTY in chunks of up to 16 KiB, calls `terminal.act(buf)`, then `network.set_current_state(terminal)`. That updates memory only. Nothing is sent until `tick()` decides to send, so any number of PTY reads collapse into one diff of *current vs. assumed*. **Intermediate frames never exist on the wire.**
+- **Client.** It renders only `get_latest_remote_state()`.
+- **Consequences** [paper]:
+  - A flooding process (`cat bigfile`, `yes`) cannot fill network buffers, so Ctrl-C keeps working.
+  - The price is that scrolled-past output is lost, so there is no accurate scrollback (§6).
+
+### 3.4 Screen diff algorithm [src terminaldisplay.cc Display::new_frame]
+
+- **Copy-on-write rows.** Rows are `shared_ptr<Row>` with a `gen` counter. An unchanged row costs one pointer comparison. This is also what keeps the full-framebuffer `operator==`, run on every timer evaluation, cheap.
+- **Non-cell state** is emitted only when it changes, or on the first frame: title (OSC), reverse video, size and scroll region, cursor visibility, SGR, OSC 8 hyperlinks, bracketed paste, mouse, focus and encoding modes.
+- **Scroll detection.** The algorithm finds the old row that matches new row 0. It then emits newlines, inside a DECSTBM region if needed, and diffs the cells against the post-scroll image.
+- **Per row.**
+  - Cells equal to the old cell are skipped.
+  - Runs of more than 4 identical blank cells use ECH (`CSI n X`).
+  - Cursor movement is kept silent.
+  - When writing the last column of a wrapping row, the diff deliberately trashes the cursor position, so the result is the same under either autowrap interpretation.
+- **Verification.** In verbose mode the sender round-trips each diff on a copy of the assumed state and reports any mismatch.
+
+### 3.5 Timing constants and pacing [src transportsender.h; paper]
+
+| Constant | Value | Role |
+|---|---|---|
+| `SEND_INTERVAL_MIN` / `MAX` | 20 / 250 ms | frame interval `= clamp(ceil(SRTT/2), 20, 250)`; "roughly two frames per RTT"; the paper describes it as "about one Instruction in flight". The 50 Hz cap is "roughly the limit of human perception" [paper]. |
+| `SEND_MINDELAY` | 8 ms on the server; the client sets 1 ms (`stmclient.cc:265`) | collection interval after the first change, so bursts clump together. The paper says it was first guessed at 15 ms and then tuned by trace sweep to 8 ms, "the minimum of the curve". |
+| `ACK_DELAY` | 100 ms | delayed ack; piggybacked on host data in more than 99.9% of cases [paper] |
+| `ACK_INTERVAL` | 3000 ms | empty-ack heartbeat |
+| `ACTIVE_RETRY_TIMEOUT` | 10 000 ms | retransmit at frame rate only while the peer has been heard within 10 s |
+| `SHUTDOWN_RETRIES` | 16 | |
+
+`calculate_timers()` works in tiers:
+1. **New data.** Send at `max(first_change + mindelay, last_send + send_interval)`.
+2. **Sent but not assumed received.** Resend at `last_send + send_interval`.
+3. **Assumed but not acked.** Retransmit at `last_send + RTO + ACK_DELAY`.
+4. **Otherwise.** Nothing is scheduled.
+
+Two extra rules apply:
+- A pending data ack pulls the ack timer to `now + 100 ms`.
+- After any send, the ack timer is reset to `now + 3 s`.
+
+### 3.6 RTT, RTO and congestion [src network.cc; paper]
+
+- **Estimator.** RFC 6298 with alpha = 1/8 and beta = 1/4. Initial values are SRTT = 1000 and RTTVAR = 500; the first sample sets SRTT = R and RTTVAR = R/2. Samples of 5000 ms or more are ignored ("server was Ctrl-Zed").
+- **RTO.** `ceil(SRTT + 4·RTTVAR)` clamped to **[50, 1000] ms**. TCP's floor is 1 s.
+- **Timestamp echo with hold-time correction.** The echoed value is `saved_ts + (now − saved_at)`, and only if `saved_ts` is less than 1 s old. Each timestamp is echoed once. Delayed acks therefore do not inflate RTT, and unique seqs remove Karn ambiguity.
+- **ECN.** Sockets set ECT(0) (`IP_TOS = 0x02`). On a CE mark, the receiver subtracts `CONGESTION_TIMESTAMP_PENALTY = 500 ms` from the timestamp it echoes. The peer's SRTT inflates and its frame rate drops toward 4 Hz. **This and the SRTT/2 frame interval are the only congestion control.** There is no cwnd.
+
