+## 4. Speculative local echo (prediction)
+
+All of this is from [src terminaloverlay.{h,cc}, stmclient.cc, completeterminal.{h,cc}] unless marked otherwise.
+
+### 4.1 Architecture
+
+- **Predictions are a display overlay, never synced state.** Each client frame:
+  1. copies the authoritative remote framebuffer;
+  2. runs `predictions.cull(fb)` (validate), then `predictions.apply(fb)` (draw), then the notification bar and title;
+  3. diffs the result against what is on the real terminal and writes the minimal update.
+
+  A wrong prediction is simply not re-applied, and the next diff paints the truth over it.
+- **Input path** (`process_user_input`):
+  - It calls `set_local_frame_sent(net.get_sent_state_last())` first.
+  - It then passes each byte to `new_user_byte(byte, local_framebuffer)`, using the *displayed* framebuffer.
+  - A `read()` of more than 100 bytes resets the engine and skips prediction (paste guard).
+  - A resize or a framebuffer size change also resets the engine.
+- **Feedback** (`process_network_input`): `set_local_frame_acked`, `set_send_interval`, and `set_local_frame_late_acked(echo_ack)`.
+
+### 4.2 Confirmation: the server echo ack
+
+- Each prediction is tagged with `expiration_frame = local_frame_sent + 1`, the client→server state number that will carry the keystroke.
+- **Server side** (`Complete`, `ECHO_TIMEOUT = 50 ms`):
+  - When input from remote state N is applied, the server calls `register_input_frame(N, now)`.
+  - `set_echo_ack(now)` advances `echo_ack` to the newest input frame that reached the PTY at least 50 ms ago.
+  - `echo_ack` is part of the synced state, so a change produces a diff. `wait_time()` wakes the server at the next deadline.
+- **Validation compares contents only, not attributes.** It is gated on `late_ack ≥ expiration_frame`; the `early_ack` argument is unused. The possible results:
+  - `Pending`: the echo ack has not reached the prediction yet.
+  - `Correct`: the content matches.
+  - `CorrectNoCredit`: the cell was unknown, the prediction was blank, or the prediction matched content that was already on screen.
+  - `IncorrectOrExpired`: anything else.
+  - A cursor prediction is `Correct` only if the server cursor is exactly at (row, col).
+- **Why it is designed this way** [paper]:
+  - Client-only checks flickered, because apps can take tens of ms to echo.
+  - Client-side timeouts broke under jitter.
+  - With a 50 ms timeout on the server, "the client has no timeouts of its own".
+  - The cost: the server "often sends an extra datagram 50 ms after a keystroke".
+
+### 4.3 When predictions are shown (adaptive mode)
+
+**Resolved contradiction.** The limits note said predictions show "once SRTT exceeds 30 ms". The prediction note said the thresholds are compared against `send_interval`. Re-checking `terminaloverlay.cc:434–444` confirms the comparison is `if (send_interval > SRTT_TRIGGER_HIGH)` and `send_interval > FLAG_TRIGGER_HIGH`. Since `send_interval = clamp(ceil(SRTT/2), 20, 250)`, the effective RTT thresholds are about **double** the constants, despite the `SRTT_` prefix in the name.
+
+| Trigger | `send_interval` | Approximate SRTT |
+|---|---|---|
+| Show predictions | > 30 ms | > ~60 ms |
+| Stop showing (only when no predictions are outstanding) | ≤ 20 ms | ≤ ~40 ms |
+| Underline on | > 80 ms | > ~160 ms |
+| Underline off | ≤ 50 ms | ≤ ~100 ms |
+
+- **Startup.** Before the first RTT sample, `send_interval` is 250, so predictions show and are underlined from the start.
+- **Glitch trigger.**
+  - A prediction still pending after 250 ms sets `glitch_trigger = 10`, so predictions show even on a fast link.
+  - One still pending after 5 s sets it to 20, which also forces underlining.
+  - Each quick (< 250 ms) correct confirmation decrements it, at most once every 150 ms, so recovery takes about 1.5 s or more.
+- **Timer.** `wait_time()` returns 50 ms while predictions are active, so glitch detection runs even with no network traffic.
+- **Validation runs even while predictions are hidden** (any mode except Never), so confidence builds in the background.
+- **Underlining** [paper]: "trails behind the user's cursor and disappears gradually as responses arrive".
+
+### 4.4 Epochs: the confidence mechanism
+
+- Predictions are grouped into epochs, "with the intention that either all of the predictions in an epoch will be correct, or none will" [paper].
+- A new epoch is **tentative**, meaning computed but not drawn, until one of its predictions is confirmed. Then the whole epoch, and every older one, becomes visible (`confirmed_epoch = max(...)`).
+- **`become_tentative()`, which starts a new epoch, fires on:**
+  - any C0 control character, including CR;
+  - any ESC dispatch;
+  - any CSI except `C`/`D` (right/left arrow);
+  - a wide or combining character (never predicted);
+  - typing in, or wrapping past, the last column.
+- `ESC O x` is rewritten to `ESC [ x`, so arrow prediction works in both cursor modes.
+- **Result:** password prompts and modal editors are handled with no special cases.
+  - After Enter at a `passwd` prompt, the following keystrokes land in a tentative epoch. The server never echoes them, so they are never confirmed and never shown, and the epoch is killed quietly.
+  - vi normal mode after ESC works the same way.
+  - [paper] This works "even with full-screen programs (like emacs) that put the terminal driver in raw mode".
+
+### 4.5 What is predicted
+
+- **Printable width-1 characters.**
+  - Insert mode (the default) shifts cells right to the edge. The last column, and any cell after an unknown one, becomes `unknown`. Original contents are recorded.
+  - Overwrite mode (`--predict-overwrite`) changes only the cursor cell.
+  - Attributes copy the cell to the left.
+  - The cursor moves right, or wraps into a new epoch.
+- **Backspace** moves the cursor left and shifts cells left (or blanks the cell in overwrite mode). It does not bump the epoch.
+- **Left/right arrows** move the cursor only.
+- **CR** predicts col 0 on the next row, in a new epoch. On the bottom row, scrolling is not predicted; the row is predicted blank.
+- **Not predicted:** up/down, Tab, Ctrl keys, Delete (`CSI 3~`), wide and combining characters, scrolling, and navigation keys such as `n` in a mail reader [paper].
+
+### 4.6 Misprediction repair (`cull`)
+
+- **Wrong and still tentative** (never shown): `kill_epoch(epoch)` drops that epoch and newer ones, starts a fresh cursor prediction at the real cursor, and makes the engine tentative. The user sees nothing.
+- **Wrong and already displayed:** a full `reset()`. The next diff overwrites the bad echo, so mistakes are "removed within an RTT" [paper].
+- **Wrong newest cursor prediction:** a full `reset()`.
+- **Correct cell:** copies the server's real attributes onto that cell and every later predicted cell in the row, so colour and bold follow what the app actually drew.
+- **Measured error rate** [paper]: 0.9% of keystrokes showed an erroneous prediction, mostly from word-wrap.
+
+### 4.7 Display modes [src, man]
+
+- **`adaptive`** (the default) uses the triggers in §4.3.
+- **`always`** skips the RTT gate but keeps the epoch gate. [man] "only show predictions when it is confident".
+- **`never`** disables prediction.
+- **`experimental`** effectively removes epochs. On a misprediction it resets only the bad cell. mosh.pl describes it as "aggressively echo even when incorrect".
+- **Configuration:** `--predict=…`, `-a`/`-n`, `MOSH_PREDICTION_DISPLAY`, and `-o` / `MOSH_PREDICTION_OVERWRITE`.
+
