+
+## 7. Successors and alternatives
+
+| System | Transport | State model | Prediction | Reattach / roam | Verified level |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **Eternal Terminal** | TCP:2022, libsodium secretbox, SSH bootstrap with passkey | raw byte stream; resend from sequence number via `BackedWriter`/`BackedReader` `CatchupBuffer` (64 MiB buffers; PTY backpressure while disconnected; `forceReset` with new salt as escape hatch) | none | reconnect, no migration; keepalive-driven | [src] |
+| **RoSE** | QUIC streams plus RFC 9221 datagrams; mTLS or SSH cert bootstrap | wezterm emulator on the server; scrollback on a separate low-priority stream | yes | 1-RTT reconnect | [2nd], early-stage |
+| **oxutrm** | QUIC, ALPN `oxutrm/1`, UDP/443; SSH-pinned SPKI and PSK per attach | host-authoritative diffs; pure sync engine tested against drop, dup and reorder; pacing `clamp(rtt/2, 8, 100) ms` | **none** | standby path takes over in ~3 s; NAT ladder (direct → PCP/UPnP → STUN → birthday → SSH tunnel); re-SSH after 20 s; holds input typed during an outage and asks whether to send it | [2nd] |
+| **WezTerm mux** | SSH domain (remote mux daemon over SSH), or TLS domain (SSH-bootstrapped cert, TLS/TCP, reconnects) | mux PDU protocol | `local_echo_threshold_ms` | TLS domain survives interruptions | [doc]; repair logic undocumented |
+| **Zellij web** (0.43/0.44) | 2 WebSockets, hashed tokens, HTTPS | server renders | none | `zellij attach https://…` | [doc] |
+| **SSH3** | QUIC (migration) | byte stream | none (per blog) | QUIC migration | [2nd] |
+| Others | qsh, morsh (QUIC/TLS 1.3), mosh-rs and morsh-n3fariox (mosh-1.4 wire-compatible; mosh-rs adds opt-in scrollback), quosh (WebTransport) | | | | [2nd] |
+
+Two other reference points:
+- **tmux** has no network transport. `-CC` control mode is an escape-sequence protocol over any pipe.
+- **VS Code Remote-SSH** (from memory, unverified) runs a remote server with a reconnection grace period. It is not a latency design.
+
+**Take-away.** ET and mosh each cover half the problem:
+- ET is reliable and complete but replays everything, with no prediction or migration.
+- Mosh is latest-wins and fast but lossy for history and has no channels.
+
+The QUIC successors try to get both by combining datagrams (latest-wins) with streams (reliable). The WezTerm mux is the closest structural analogue to herdr: an existing mux PDU protocol extended with local echo and a reconnecting TLS transport.
+
