+
+## 8. Design lessons for a multiplexer with an existing client/server split
+
+**Premise [inf].** A multiplexer server already holds the authoritative emulator and history; for herdr that is libghostty-vt. That is mosh's hardest component, and it removes mosh's two biggest weaknesses: lost scrollback and no reattach. The missing pieces are:
+- a loss- and address-change-tolerant transport;
+- idempotent latest-wins screen sync;
+- client-side prediction.
+
+**Open question.** Whether this is a transport swap or a protocol change depends on how herdr frames screen updates today. That is the herdr-side investigation, not covered here.
+
+Ranked by impact relative to effort:
+
+| # | Idea | Impact | Effort | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | **Latest-wins screen sync with frame skipping**: numbered per-pane (or per-client-view) states; send `diff(assumed/acked → current)` with `old/new/ack/throwaway` semantics; drop intermediate frames | Very high: keeps Ctrl-C responsive under floods, bounds catch-up after any outage to one diff, and works over the *existing* reliable transport too | Medium | Needs cheap snapshot equality and diffs. Mosh relies on copy-on-write `shared_ptr` rows plus a `gen` counter; check whether libghostty-vt offers row identity or dirty tracking. Keep a few reference states (mosh: 32 sent, 1024 received). |
+| 2 | **RTT-paced frame interval plus collection delay**: `clamp(SRTT/2, 20, 250)` ms, 8 ms collection on the server and ~1 ms on the client, 100 ms delayed ack, 3 s heartbeat | High: one frame in flight, no self-inflicted bufferbloat | Low | Needs RTT measurement: hold-time-corrected timestamp echo, RFC 6298 estimator, RTO in [50, 1000]. Useful even over TCP/SSH. |
+| 3 | **Asymmetric directions**: input as a reliable ordered log trimmed on ack; output as latest-state | High: correctness | Low | Mosh resends the whole unacked input suffix in every diff. Never drop or reorder keystrokes. |
+| 4 | **Reattach via a server-held session secret**: key plus epoch or resume token in server session state; fresh nonce space per attach | High: fixes mosh's worst gap; a new client process resumes | Low–medium | Bootstrap over existing SSH, mosh/ET/oxutrm style: print port, key and fingerprint, then detach. Replay protection: retarget only on an authentic, strictly newer seq. |
+| 5 | **Two traffic classes**: latest-wins (screen diffs, cursor, echo ack) and reliable (input, resize, clipboard/OSC 52, notifications, titles, file transfer, future forwarding) | High: avoids mosh's dropped and coalesced side-channel events and its channel-less dead end | Medium | Model side-channel sequences as typed reliable events, not per-frame compared state (mosh coalesces identical OSC 52 copies). Design extensible channels now, even if forwarding ships later. |
+| 6 | **Scrollback as a separate reliable, lazy, low-priority stream** keyed by absolute line number | High for UX | Medium | RoSE approach [2nd]. The server already owns history. Avoids both mosh's loss and ET's 64 MiB replay flood. |
+| 7 | **Datagram transport with roaming**: authenticated UDP (AEAD, direction bit plus seq nonce), retarget on newest authentic seq, client port hop after 10 s without a round trip, 3 s keepalive | High on mobile and Wi-Fi roaming | Medium–high if hand-rolled | Alternative: QUIC (connection migration, PLPMTUD, congestion control, streams plus datagrams, UDP/443). **Not evaluated in these notes; needs its own spike.** Fragment carefully: mosh's single-assembly reassembler is fragile for large repaints, so use ~1200 B datagrams or let QUIC handle it. |
+| 8 | **Keep the SSH-tunnelled transport as a fallback** running the same sync semantics | Medium–high: UDP is often blocked (mosh #13, #970) | Low if #1 is transport-agnostic | Loses migration and keeps idempotent catch-up. oxutrm's NAT ladder ends in an SSH tunnel for the same reason [2nd]. |
+| 9 | **Predictive local echo as a client overlay** | High on > 60 ms links, mostly for plain typing | High | Requirements: a client-side emulator copy matching the server; numbered input frames; a server-computed per-pane **echo ack** ("input N has been in the PTY ≥ 50 ms") in each frame; epoch gating (bump on control, ESC or non-arrow CSI); content-only validation; kill or reset on a miss; gate on `send_interval > 30` (SRTT ≳ 60 ms) and underline above 80 (SRTT ≳ 160 ms); glitch triggers at 250 ms / 5 s; skip reads over 100 B (paste); reset on resize; pane-local coordinates. **Risk:** the paper's 70% instant / 0.9% wrong figures come from 2012 bash/emacs/pine traces. Modern TUIs (zsh syntax highlighting and autosuggest, fzf, agent TUIs) will probably yield less (unverified). Ship last, behind a flag. |
+| 10 | **Outage UX** | Medium | Low | "Last contact N s" banner (mosh 6.5 s / 10 s thresholds); optionally hold input typed during an outage and confirm before sending (oxutrm). Server panes must keep running headless indefinitely: do **not** copy mosh's idle-timeout semantics as defaults, and keep output collapsing into state, not into a queue. |
+| 11 | **ECN-based slowdown** | Low | Low | Mosh subtracts 500 ms from the echoed timestamp on CE. Marginal. Only worth it if rolling a custom UDP layer (QUIC handles congestion itself). |
+
+**Suggested sequencing [inf].**
+1. Ship #1–#3 over the existing transport first. That is pure protocol work, testable against simulated drop, duplicate and reorder, as oxutrm does.
+2. Then #4 and #5.
+3. Then #7 with #8, choosing between QUIC and a custom layer.
+4. Prediction (#9) last.
+
